.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt PS 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ps
.Nd process status
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl AaCcefHjlmrSTuvwXx
.Op Fl O Ar fmt | Fl o Ar fmt
.Op Fl G Ar gid Ns Op , Ns Ar gid Ns ...
.Op Fl p Ar pid Ns Op , Ns Ar pid Ns ...
.Op Fl t Ar tty Ns Op , Ns Ar tty Ns ...
.Op Fl U Ar user Ns Op , Ns Ar user Ns ...
.Nm
.Fl L
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints a header line and then a line for each process selected.
By default those are the processes whose effective user is the caller and
that have a controlling terminal, in the columns
.Cm pid , tt , state , time
and
.Cm command ,
sorted by terminal and then process ID.
The information comes from
.Xr procfs 5 .
.Pp
The leading
.Ql -
of the first argument may be left out, as in
.Ql ps aux ,
and a process ID given as an operand is taken as if by
.Fl p .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A
All processes: the same as
.Fl ax .
.It Fl a
Processes of other users too.
.It Fl C
Accepted for compatibility: there is only the one CPU figure.
.It Fl c
Show only the command name in the
.Cm command
column, not the arguments.
.It Fl e
Show the environment after the arguments, where the system makes it known.
.It Fl f
Accepted for compatibility: no process is ever swapped out.
.It Fl G Ar gid
Processes whose real group is one of those listed, by name or number.
.It Fl H
Accepted for compatibility: each process is shown as one thread.
.It Fl j
The columns
.Cm user , pid , ppid , pgid , sid , jobc , state , tt , time
and
.Cm command .
.It Fl L
List the keywords
.Fl o
knows, and exit.
.It Fl l
The columns
.Cm uid , pid , ppid , cpu , pri , nice , vsz , rss , mwchan , state , tt , time
and
.Cm command .
.It Fl m
Sort by resident memory, largest first.
.It Fl O Ar fmt
Add the keywords in
.Ar fmt
after the
.Cm pid
column of the format in use.
.It Fl o Ar fmt
Show the columns named in
.Ar fmt ,
a list of keywords separated by commas or blanks.
A keyword may be followed by
.Ql =
and a header for its column, which runs to the end of the argument;
if every header is empty, no header line is printed.
.Fl o
may be given more than once.
.It Fl p Ar pid
Processes with the process IDs listed.
.It Fl r
Sort by CPU use, largest first.
.It Fl S
Count the CPU time of a process's waited-for children in its
.Cm time .
.It Fl T
Processes on the terminal of the standard input.
.It Fl t Ar tty
Processes whose controlling terminal is one of those listed:
a full name
.Pq Pa ttyv0 , Pa /dev/ttyv0 ,
the abbreviation
.Cm tt
shows
.Pq Ql v0 ,
or
.Ql -
for processes without one.
.It Fl U Ar user
Processes whose real user is one of those listed, by name or number.
.It Fl u
The columns
.Cm user , pid , %cpu , %mem , vsz , rss , tt , state , start , time
and
.Cm command ;
implies
.Fl r .
.It Fl v
The columns
.Cm pid , state , time , sl , re , pagein , vsz , rss , lim , tsiz , %cpu , %mem
and
.Cm command ;
implies
.Fl m .
.It Fl w
Lines are cut to the width of the terminal;
.Fl w
makes that at least 132 columns, and
.Fl ww
does not cut them.
Output that is not to a terminal is never cut.
.It Fl X
With other selections, leave out processes without a controlling terminal.
.It Fl x
Include processes without a controlling terminal.
.El
.Pp
The selections
.Fl G , p , t , T
and
.Fl U
add up: a process any of them names is shown.
.Fl G , p
and
.Fl U
imply
.Fl x
unless
.Fl X
is given.
Of
.Fl j , l , u
and
.Fl v ,
each given adds its columns.
.Ss Keywords
The keywords, their headers and what they show; aliases are listed after
the keyword they stand for.
Values the system doesn't keep are shown as
.Ql - .
.Bl -tag -width "sigignore"
.It Cm %cpu
.Pq Cm pcpu ; %CPU
CPU time used, as a percentage of the time since the process started.
.It Cm %mem
.Pq Cm pmem ; %MEM
Resident memory as a percentage of the machine's.
.It Cm args
.Pq Cm command ; COMMAND
The command and its arguments; a process without any shows its name in
brackets, a zombie
.Ql <defunct> .
.It Cm comm
.Pq COMMAND
The command name.
.It Cm etime
.Pq ELAPSED
Time since the process started,
.Sm off
.Oo Oo Ar dd Li \&- Oc Ar hh Li \&: Oc Ar mm Li \&: Ar ss ;
.Sm on
.Cm etimes
the same in seconds.
.It Cm f
.Pq Cm flags ; F
The process flags, in hexadecimal.
.It Cm gid , rgid , svgid
.Pq GID , RGID , SVGID
The effective, real and saved group IDs;
.Cm group
and
.Cm rgroup
the names of the first two.
.It Cm lstart
.Pq STARTED
The time the process started, in full.
.It Cm majflt , minflt
.Pq MAJFLT , MINFLT ; Cm pagein
Page faults that did and did not read from a disk.
.It Cm nice
.Pq Cm ni ; NI
The nice value.
.It Cm nlwp
.Pq NLWP
The number of threads.
.It Cm pgid , pid , ppid , sid
.Pq PGID , PID , PPID , SID
The process group, process, parent process and session IDs.
.It Cm pri
.Pq PRI
The scheduling priority:
20 and the nice value for a time-sharing process, below zero for a
real-time one;
.Cm rtprio
shows
.Ql normal
or
.Ql real: Ns Ar n .
.It Cm rss
.Pq Cm rssize , rsz ; RSS
Resident memory, in kilobytes.
.It Cm sigcatch , sigignore , sigmask , pending
.Pq CAUGHT , IGNORED , BLOCKED , PENDING
Signal masks, in hexadecimal.
.It Cm start
.Pq STARTED
When the process started: the time of day within the last day, the
weekday and hour within the last week, else the date.
.It Cm state
.Pq Cm stat ; STAT
The state: one of
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Li D
waiting for a disk or another uninterruptible event
.It Li I
idle (a Linux kernel thread)
.It Li R
runnable
.It Li S
sleeping
.It Li T
stopped
.It Li Z
a zombie, dead and not yet waited for
.El
and then any of
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Li <
raised priority
.It Li N
lowered priority
.It Li V
a
.Xr vfork 2
child still borrowing its parent's memory
.It Li s
a session leader
.It Li +
in the foreground process group of its terminal
.El
.It Cm systime , usertime , time
.Pq SYSTIME , USERTIME , TIME ; Cm cputime
CPU time in the system, in the program, and both, as
.Ar minutes : Ns Ar seconds . Ns Ar hundredths .
.It Cm tdev
.Pq TDEV
The controlling terminal's device number.
.It Cm tpgid , tsid
.Pq TPGID , TSID
The foreground process group and the session of the controlling terminal.
.It Cm tt , tty
.Pq TT , TTY
The controlling terminal, abbreviated
.Pf ( Pa ttyv0
as
.Ql v0 ,
.Pa pts/1
as
.Ql 1 )
or in full.
.It Cm uid , ruid , svuid
.Pq Cm euid ; UID , RUID , SVUID
The effective, real and saved user IDs;
.Cm user
and
.Cm ruser
the names of the first two.
.It Cm wchan
.Pq Cm mwchan ; WCHAN
What a sleeping process waits on.
.El
.Pp
Also known, and shown as
.Ql - :
.Cm acflag , class , cow , cpu , dsiz , emul , inblk , jobc , ktrace ,
.Cm label , lim , lockname , login , msgrcv , msgsnd , nivcsw , nsigs ,
.Cm nswap , nvcsw , nwchan , oublk , paddr , re , sl , ssiz , tdaddr ,
.Cm tdnam , tracer , tsiz , upr , uprocp
and
.Cm xstat .
.Cm jid
is always 0, and
.Cm lwp
and
.Cm tid
are the process ID.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/proc/<pid>/map" -compact
.It Pa /proc
the processes,
.Xr procfs 5
.It Pa /dev
terminals' names
.It Pa /etc/passwd , Pa /etc/group
user and group names
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if it showed a process, 1 if it showed none or something failed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every process, with its user and memory use:
.Dl $ ps aux
.Pp
The process tree's IDs for one process:
.Dl $ ps -o pid,ppid,pgid,sid,command -p 1
.Pp
The commands of a user's processes, without a header:
.Dl $ ps -U operator -o comm=
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr pgrep 1 ,
.Xr procfs 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
follows the BSDs.
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq)
gives
.Fl A , G , o , p , t
and
.Fl U
the same meaning; its
.Fl e ,
.Fl f ,
.Fl g ,
.Fl l
and
.Fl n
are not those of
.Nm .
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v3 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh BUGS
The system keeps no decaying average of CPU use, so
.Cm %cpu
is the average over the process's life, and no sleep time, so a long
sleeper shows
.Li S ,
not
.Li I .
